{
  "title": "Binary Tree Level Order Traversal",
  "description": "Print the tree's values level by level, left to right.",
  "statement": "A warehouse index stores goods in a binary hierarchy, and the front-end has to render that hierarchy as a flat grid.\n\nTraverse the tree breadth-first: every node of one level is visited before any node of the next level, and within a level the nodes are visited from left to right. Report the node values in exactly that order, one value per line.\n\nA queue is the natural structure: enqueue the root, and for every level drain the current queue completely while enqueuing the non-missing children, which are then the entire next level. Placeholders in the input (missing children) must not be printed - only real nodes are.",
  "inputFormat": "The input is read from standard input as exactly one line:\n\n1. Line 1: the tree in level-order (breadth-first) serialisation. Values are separated by commas with **no spaces**, and the literal `null` marks a missing child. The tree always has at least one node (up to `10^5` nodes).",
  "outputFormat": "Print one node value per line in breadth-first order. The output contains exactly as many lines as there are nodes - no blanks, no separators.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= number of nodes <= 10^5",
    "-10^4 <= node value <= 10^4",
    "The level-order line contains no spaces around the commas"
  ],
  "notes": "Values repeat, so the output can legitimately contain duplicates such as four 4s. Missing children appear in the input as `null` and must not appear in the output at all - printing them shifts every later line. Output is one value per line, not one line per level, so a level such as `2 3` must be printed as two separate lines. The 1023-node case makes a recursive solution risky in languages with small stacks, so an iterative queue is the safer shape.",
  "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
  "tags": [
    "tree",
    "queue",
    "breadth-first-search"
  ],
  "timeLimitMs": 1000,
  "memoryLimitKb": 256000,
  "starterCode": {
    "python": "import sys\n\ndef main():\n    levelOrder = sys.stdin.readline().strip()\n    # TODO: print the result\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n",
    "javascript": "const fs = require('fs');\nconst levelOrder = fs.readFileSync(0, 'utf8').trim();\nconst tokens = levelOrder.length === 0 ? [] : levelOrder.split(',');\n// TODO: print the result\n",
    "java": "import java.io.BufferedReader;\nimport java.io.InputStreamReader;\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        String line = br.readLine();\n        List<String> tokens = new ArrayList<>();\n        if (line != null && !line.isEmpty()) {\n            for (String part : line.split(\",\")) {\n                tokens.add(part.trim());\n            }\n        }\n        // TODO: print the result\n    }\n}\n",
    "cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n\n    string line;\n    getline(cin, line);\n    vector<string> tokens;\n    stringstream ss(line);\n    string token;\n    while (getline(ss, token, ',')) {\n        tokens.push_back(token);\n    }\n\n    // TODO: print the result\n    return 0;\n}\n"
  },
  "source": "Original",
